                <pb facs="0420" n="332" sameAs="frus:frus1904/pg332"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hay to Mr. White.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1904-04-20"><hi rendition="#italic">April 20,
                        1904</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>(Mr. Hay states that the Russian ambassador has given notice to the Department
                    that correspondents comunicating war news by wireless telegraph off the coast of
                    Kwantung or within the zone of operations of the Russian fleet will be treated
                    as spies, and that vessels with such apparatus will be seized. In taking note of
                    this declaration the Department adds that the United States Government does not
                    waive any right it may have in international law should any American citizen be
                    arrested or any American vessel be seized.)</p>
            </div>
